<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Amida (359)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">359 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-persian-wars">Roman–Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> in 359 CE was the capture of the Roman city of Amida, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a>, by the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> profile, the city fell after a 73-day siege. The siege was described by the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, who was present.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> sought to regain territory lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. After a decade of war against the Xionites he made peace with them, and their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grumbates">Grumbates</a> joined him against the Romans. In 359 he invaded while Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> was occupied in the west. Constantius had come to doubt the loyalty of the general Ursicinus and gave the eastern command to Sabinianus, but Ursicinus, on whose staff <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> served, moved his headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, where several legions gathered beside the garrison of Legio V Parthica.</p><p>Shapur meant to bypass fortress cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> and march into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, but he turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, where his vanguard nearly captured Ursicinus. When the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grumbates">Grumbates</a> was killed by an arrow from the walls while inspecting the defences, the Sassanids attacked the city; Ammianus likens the death to that of Patroclus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Early in the siege 70 Persian archers, helped by a Roman renegade, entered a tower on the south side and shot into the city until their arrows ran out and they were killed. Assaults on the walls were driven back and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> burned. Albanian, Saka and Chionite cavalry ringed the city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> were used on the west, and a plague in the city ended after ten days with a light rain. Two Gallic legions made a night sortie that inflicted heavy losses on the Persian camp.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> then raised earth mounds against the walls, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built higher mounds inside. When one of these collapsed, the attackers reached the ramparts and broke in. A massacre followed, and Count Aelianus, who had directed the defence, was crucified with his surviving officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The city was sacked and its people deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>. With autumn come, Shapur could advance no further; he had lost as many as 30,000 men and his eastern allies deserted him. In 360 he took Bezabde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singara">Singara</a>, and after Emperor Julian was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 363 his successor Jovian made a peace that ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> and the districts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. The historian Khodadad Rezakhani has suggested that the king whom Ammianus describes before the walls, wearing a ram's head, may have been the Kidarite ruler Peroz.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Amida (359)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
